{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-contradiction-3425", "original": "The census of running writers turned up a divergence we had not expected. Seven processes write the shared sidecar for this project, and six of them run from unlinked binaries, so their code differs from the checked-out tree. All seven read the same endpoint. ⟦Reading /proc/<pid>/environ for each showed that none of them sets a model variable, so every process falls back to its compiled-in default protocol.⟧ Yet the launch logs show three of them speaking `openai` and four speaking `llama-server` at the same moment. The divergence is therefore not only in built code but in live behaviour between processes.", "substituted": "The census of running writers turned up a divergence we had not expected. Seven processes write the shared sidecar for this project, and six of them run from unlinked binaries, so their code differs from the checked-out tree. All seven read the same endpoint. ⟦Reading /proc/<pid>/environ for each showed that none of them sets a model variable, so the protocol split must come from the binaries' compiled-in defaults.⟧ Yet the launch logs show three of them speaking `openai` and four speaking `llama-server` at the same moment. The divergence is therefore not only in built code but in live behaviour between processes."}
{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-contradiction-6604", "original": "The reset guard landed as a small shell script with its own test file, copied in structure from the formatter guard because both protect a shared git index that nobody owns. It refuses `--mixed` and `--hard` outright, with no force flag and no acknowledgement variable, since no situation exists where either mode is safe once someone acknowledges the risk. The refusal message lists how many files are staged, their names, and who wrote them. A reset with zero staged files still refuses, because a peer's `git add` can land between the check and the reset. ⟦Any caller may pass an acknowledgement variable to proceed when the refusal is expected.⟧ With no prior implementation there was no failing test to start from, so I blanked the count line and saw 34 of 35 pass, exactly the count assertion.", "substituted": "The reset guard landed as a small shell script with its own test file, copied in structure from the formatter guard because both protect a shared git index that nobody owns. It refuses `--mixed` and `--hard` outright, with no force flag and no acknowledgement variable, since no situation exists where either mode is safe once someone acknowledges the risk. The refusal message lists how many files are staged, their names, and who wrote them. A reset with zero staged files still refuses, because a peer's `git add` can land between the check and the reset. ⟦No caller may pass an acknowledgement variable to proceed, even when the refusal is expected.⟧ With no prior implementation there was no failing test to start from, so I blanked the count line and saw 34 of 35 pass, exactly the count assertion."}
{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-contradiction-7296", "original": "Phase 1 of the storage split is done. I extracted a vector-store trait covering collection setup, chunk refs, upsert, delete, query and index stats, and the Qdrant wrapper is its first implementation. The retrieval client now holds a trait object instead of the concrete Qdrant handle, and semantic search and project sync both go through it. The memory store and artifact store build their own wrappers, so nothing outside the client touches its internals any more. An in-memory brute-force cosine store pins the contract, and the sqlite-vec implementation must pass the same tests. ⟦Because CI has no Qdrant daemon, a live parity test against the trait runs on every CI build.⟧ The contract test stands in for it, and the sqlite-vec work will rerun that.", "substituted": "Phase 1 of the storage split is done. I extracted a vector-store trait covering collection setup, chunk refs, upsert, delete, query and index stats, and the Qdrant wrapper is its first implementation. The retrieval client now holds a trait object instead of the concrete Qdrant handle, and semantic search and project sync both go through it. The memory store and artifact store build their own wrappers, so nothing outside the client touches its internals any more. An in-memory brute-force cosine store pins the contract, and the sqlite-vec implementation must pass the same tests. ⟦Because CI has no Qdrant daemon, a live parity test against the trait cannot run on any CI build.⟧ The contract test stands in for it, and the sqlite-vec work will rerun that."}
{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-contradiction-8412", "original": "Error handling for the call graph tool follows the existing tiers. A missing symbol returns a recoverable error with a hint to use the symbol lookup tool for candidates. When neither an LSP nor a tree-sitter parser exists for the language, the tool returns a recoverable error naming the language. Timeouts and open circuit breakers are surfaced by the LSP layer, and we simply propagate them. A cache write failure is logged and the call continues, since the graph is already computed and persistence is best-effort. ⟦A failed cache write aborts the call with a fatal error so the operator notices.⟧ Genuine bugs such as panics, schema corruption and broken internal invariants bail out as fatal.", "substituted": "Error handling for the call graph tool follows the existing tiers. A missing symbol returns a recoverable error with a hint to use the symbol lookup tool for candidates. When neither an LSP nor a tree-sitter parser exists for the language, the tool returns a recoverable error naming the language. Timeouts and open circuit breakers are surfaced by the LSP layer, and we simply propagate them. A cache write failure is logged and the call continues, since the graph is already computed and persistence is best-effort. ⟦A failed cache write is never fatal, and the log line is what the operator notices.⟧ Genuine bugs such as panics, schema corruption and broken internal invariants bail out as fatal."}
